<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kazakhs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kazakhs are a Turkic people of the Central Asian steppe. Their lands were progressively absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> during the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. The site notes the imposition of the Russian language on subject peoples from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> to Kazakhs as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s imperial impact.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early seventeenth century the Oirats were divided and under pressure from the Khalkha Mongols and the <b>Kazakhs</b>; some groups migrated west to the Volga, where they became the Kalmyks, and others moved to the Kokonor region and Tibet as the Khoshut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its successor states, the khanates of Kazan, Crimea, Astrakhan and Sibir, carried its political traditions into the early modern period, the Crimean Khanate surviving until 1783, and the Volga and Crimean Tatars, the <b>Kazakhs</b> and the Uzbeks all trace parts of their history to the Ulus of Jochi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The name <b>Kazakh</b> is generally explained as meaning a free or independent wanderer, a word once used of men who had broken away from their own tribe or ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Russian cultural influence, spread through the Orthodox Church, the Russian language imposed on subject peoples from Poles to <b>Kazakhs</b>, and the extraordinary literary and musical flowering of the nineteenth century represented by Pushkin, Tolstoy, Dostoevsky, Tchaikovsky and Mussorgsky, created a distinctive civilisational sphere that both drew on and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its borders changed repeatedly: Turkestan was divided in 1924, the <b>Kazakh</b> and Kirghiz autonomous republics became separate Union republics in 1936, Tuva was annexed in 1944, the Kaliningrad region was added in 1946, and Crimea was transferred to Ukraine in 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Alash party was founded by <b>Kazakh</b> intellectuals including Alikhan Bukeikhanov, Akhmet Baitursynov and Mirzhakip Dulatov.</blockquote><a href="/state/alash-autonomy">Alash Autonomy</a></li></ul></section>
